## F1 — Modules nested under `modules/` instead of top-level directories

**What we're fixing:** all five finops modules live at
`modules/finops-alerts/`, `modules/finops-budgets/`, etc. `agents.md`
requires each module to be its own top-level directory at the repo root.

**Why we need this fix:** this is a direct, explicit contradiction of the
repo's own contributor instructions. Anyone following `agents.md` step 3
before creating a new module will look for a top-level directory, not find
one, and either duplicate work or create the next module in the wrong
place too — the inconsistency compounds over time.

**How to fix:**
1. `git mv modules/finops-alerts finops-alerts` (repeat for the other 4).
2. Update every `source = "../modules/finops-*"` reference — in
   `test-finops-dataset/main.tf`, and in each module's own README usage
   example — to `source = "../finops-*"` (one level up instead of two).
3. Update any CI/lint config (`.tflint.hcl`, path-based test globs) that
   assumes a `modules/` prefix.
4. Run `terraform init` in `test-finops-dataset/` afterward — module source
   path changes require re-initializing (see F14).

**AGENTS.md reference:**
> "Check the repo root for an existing module directory covering the same or
> adjacent resource before creating a new one (each module is its own
> top-level directory, e.g. `network/`, `iam/`, `logging/` — not nested
> under a `modules/` folder)."
— `agents.md`, "Before creating or editing a module", item 3. Also restated
in the "Creating a new module" checklist: *"as its own top-level directory
at the repo root."*

**Won't break / scalable:** this is a path rename, not a logic change — no
resource addresses change, so `terraform state` is untouched (state keys
are based on resource addresses inside the module, not the module's
filesystem location, as long as the module block name in the calling root
stays the same). Safe to do as a pure `git mv` + source string update,
verified with `terraform plan` showing zero diffs afterward.

