## F2 — Duplicate budget-enforcement logic (`finops-budgets` vs. `finops-budget-controls`)

**What we're fixing:** both modules independently implement a
`google_billing_budget` resource with the same shape: `amount` block,
`dynamic "threshold_rules"`, `all_updates_rule`, `budget_filter`. The only
real difference is `finops-budget-controls` adds a `filter_projects`/scope
concept and a billing-viewer IAM grant.

**Why we need this fix:** two modules doing the same job means every future
change to budget behavior (a new provider field, a bug fix, a new
threshold type) has to be made twice, and it's easy to forget one — they
will drift apart silently. It also directly violates the "extend, don't
duplicate" guidance before creating a new module.

**How to fix:**
1. Pick `finops-budgets` as the surviving module (it's the more complete
   one, already wired to the alerts + dataset modules).
2. Add the scoping capability from `finops-budget-controls` into
   `finops-budgets`'s `budgets` object variable — e.g. add optional
   `filter_projects`, `filter_labels` (already partially present) and the
   `iam_viewers` variable + `google_billing_account_iam_member.viewer`
   resource from `finops-budget-controls`.
3. Update `test-finops-dataset/main.tf`: replace the separate
   `module "finops_budget_controls"` block by merging its `scopes` config
   into the `budgets` map passed to `module.finops_budgets`, and merge
   `budgets.yaml`'s `budget_control_scopes` key into `budgets`.
4. Delete `finops-budget-controls` entirely once callers are migrated.
5. Update `finops-budgets/README.md` to document the new scoping fields.

**AGENTS.md reference:**
> "Check the repo root for an existing module directory covering the same or
> adjacent resource before creating a new one... Extend an existing module
> if the resource fits its stated responsibility; create a new one if it
> doesn't."
— `agents.md`, item 3. Also `BEST_PRACTICES.md`: *"One module = one clear
responsibility. If a module is doing IAM, networking, and logging all at
once, split it"* — the inverse problem here (splitting one responsibility
across two modules) is the same anti-pattern in reverse.

**Won't break / scalable:** because both modules use `for_each` over a map
keyed by a caller-chosen string (`var.budgets` / `var.scopes`), merging
them means the surviving module's state simply gains more map keys —
existing `google_billing_budget.budget["monthly_overall"]` resources are
untouched as long as you don't rename that key during the merge.
Migrating `finops-budget-controls`'s existing budgets requires either a
`terraform state mv`, or a plan/apply cycle since these are still
early-stage modules with no real deployed budgets yet. Scales cleanly: one
map-driven module now covers both "one overall budget" and "N scoped
budgets" use cases without new code paths.

