## F7 — IAM is implemented as loose, ad-hoc grants instead of following CFF's two IAM patterns

**What we're fixing:** every IAM grant in this FinOps stage today is either
a loose resource in `test-finops-dataset/main.tf` (`google_project_iam_member`,
`google_billing_account_iam_member`) or missing entirely from a module that
should own it. There is no consistent, per-module IAM interface, and the
roles granted are broader than necessary (`roles/bigquery.admin`,
`roles/pubsub.admin`, `roles/monitoring.editor`, `roles/storage.admin`,
`roles/cloudfunctions.admin`, `roles/billing.admin`).

**Why we need this fix:** without a consistent pattern, IAM grants get
bolted onto whatever file is closest at the time, which is exactly how
this stage ended up with full-project admin roles self-granted by the same
Terraform run that then uses them. If this SA or its credentials are ever
compromised, the blast radius is "admin over BigQuery, Pub/Sub, Monitoring,
Storage, and Cloud Functions across the whole project," not "manage FinOps
budgets and views."

**CFF's two IAM patterns** (verified directly against CFF's own module
source, not assumed):

**Pattern A — "a resource that others need access to"**
(`project`, `bigquery-dataset`, `pubsub`): each has its own `iam.tf` +
`variables-iam.tf` exposing the same trio:
- `iam` = `map(list(string))` (role → members) → implemented via the
  **authoritative** `_iam_binding` resource (CFF treats this variable as
  "you now own this role's whole list").
- `iam_bindings` → authoritative, keyed, supports conditions.
- `iam_bindings_additive` → **additive** `_iam_member`, one grant at a
  time, safe to use alongside other automation/teams.

**Pattern B — "a resource that itself needs to act as an identity"**
(`cloud-function-v2`): no `iam.tf` at all. Instead `serviceaccount.tf` +
`variables-serviceaccount.tf` — the module creates its **own** runtime SA
and grants *that SA* roles via `service_account_config.roles` (default
`["roles/logging.logWriter","roles/monitoring.metricWriter"]`) →
`google_project_iam_member`. The direction is inverted: not "who can
access this function," but "what can this function's own SA do."

CFF does not use one pattern everywhere — Pattern A for anything other
principals need to reach into, Pattern B for anything that is itself an
identity performing actions.

**Mapping onto this repo's modules:**

| CFF module | finops equivalent | Pattern | Current state | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `bigquery-dataset` | `finops-dataset` | A | Has `variable "iam"` + `google_bigquery_dataset_iam_binding` (main.tf:40) | ✅ Already correct — matches CFF exactly, since `iam` is meant to authoritatively own that role's members, satisfying `agents.md`'s "unless the module is meant to fully own the whole binding list" carve-out. Optional: add `iam_bindings_additive` for callers who want one grant without owning the whole list. |
| `pubsub` | `finops-alerts` (Pub/Sub + notification channels) | A | No `iam.tf`, no IAM variable at all | Real gap — add the same `iam`/`iam_bindings_additive` pair, scoped to the topic/notification channel, mirroring CFF's `pubsub/iam.tf`. |
| `project` | `modules/project` | A | `activate_apis` only, no `iam.tf` | Real gap — add `variable "iam" { type = map(list(string)) }` + a `project-iam.tf` with `google_project_iam_member`. |
| `cloud-function-v2` | Cloud Function loose in `test-finops-dataset/main.tf` | B | No dedicated module, no owned runtime SA, secrets as plaintext env vars | See **F8** — extract into a `finops-function` module following Pattern B. |
| — (no CFF equivalent; budgets have no per-resource IAM surface) | `finops-budgets`/`finops-budget-controls` | A (billing-account scoped) | Already additive via `google_billing_account_iam_member` + `iam_viewers` | ✅ Already correct — GCP has no per-budget IAM resource, so billing-account-level additive grants is the right and only option, matching CFF (no per-budget `iam.tf` exists there either). |

**One deliberate, documented difference from CFF for `modules/project`'s
new `iam.tf`:** use `google_project_iam_member` (additive), not
`google_project_iam_binding` (authoritative) like CFF's own project
module. CFF's project module is meant to fully own project IAM for a
project it creates fresh. Here, the FinOps stage may be applied against a
project that already has bindings from elsewhere, so an authoritative
`_binding` would silently wipe them out on the next apply.

**Least-privilege role substitutions** (verified against GCP's predefined
role catalog — verify against your actual provider version in a sandbox
before relying on this):

| Resource | Currently granted | Actually required | Resource-scoped alternative |
|---|---|---|---|
| BigQuery dataset + views | `roles/bigquery.admin` | `roles/bigquery.dataEditor` | `google_bigquery_dataset_iam_member` scoped to the dataset, once it exists |
| Pub/Sub topic | `roles/pubsub.admin` | `roles/pubsub.editor` (`admin` only needed to `setIamPolicy`, unused here) | `google_pubsub_topic_iam_member` scoped to the topic |
| Notification channels | `roles/monitoring.editor` | `roles/monitoring.notificationChannelEditor` | N/A — Monitoring notification channels have no resource-level IAM; project-level is the finest grain, but the role itself is narrower |
| Billing budgets | `roles/billing.admin` (billing-account level) | `roles/billing.costsManager` | inherently billing-account-scoped; role itself is narrower |
| Cloud Function | `roles/cloudfunctions.admin` | `roles/cloudfunctions.developer` (`admin` also allows setting IAM policy, unused here) | `google_cloudfunctions2_function_iam_member` scoped to the function |
| GCS bucket + object upload | `roles/storage.admin` | No narrower predefined role exists for bucket *creation* — mitigate architecturally: pre-create the bucket once (outside repeated applies), then only need `roles/storage.objectAdmin` scoped to that bucket for uploads |
| Eventarc trigger | implicit — default compute SA | Dedicated trigger SA with only `roles/eventarc.eventReceiver` + `roles/run.invoker` | `roles/run.invoker` bound to the specific Cloud Run service (2nd-gen functions run on Cloud Run) |

**Service account count — 3 identities, reused across every environment**
(not one-per-resource, not one-all-admin-SA):
1. **Terraform deploy SA** (`tf-executor`) — gets the narrowed roles above,
   granted once per project via the new `iam` variable on `modules/project`.
2. **Cloud Function runtime SA** — a separate SA from `tf-executor` (today
   `service_config.service_account_email` reuses `tf-executor`, which is
   itself a smell). Only needs `roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor` (F9).
3. **Eventarc trigger SA** — `eventReceiver` + `run.invoker` only.

Environment-wise scoping is handled by GCP project boundaries, not SA
proliferation: each environment (dev/staging/prod) is its own GCP project,
and each project gets its own copy of the same three identities with the
same narrow role set applied within that project.

**How to fix:**
1. Add `variable "iam" { type = map(list(string)); default = {} }` +
   `project-iam.tf` to `modules/project` (Pattern A, additive `_member`).
2. Add the same `iam`/`iam_bindings_additive` pair to `finops-alerts`
   (Pattern A).
3. Replace each `*.admin`/`*.editor` grant in `test-finops-dataset/main.tf`
   with the narrower role from the table above, passed through the new
   `modules/project` `iam` variable instead of loose resources.
4. Build the runtime/trigger SAs and their grants inside the new
   `finops-function` module (Pattern B — see F8), not in `modules/project`.
5. Pre-create the function-source GCS bucket once, outside this stage's
   repeated applies, and drop `roles/storage.admin` to
   `roles/storage.objectAdmin` scoped to that one bucket for the deploy SA.

**AGENTS.md reference:**
> "Follow least privilege: grant the narrowest role that accomplishes the
> task, scoped to the narrowest resource level that makes sense (prefer
> project/resource-level bindings over org-level where possible)."
> "Prefer `google_*_iam_member` for additive, single-principal grants used
> alongside other automation. Use `google_*_iam_binding` only when this
> module is meant to fully own the role's membership list."
— `BEST_PRACTICES.md`, "IAM and security". CFF's `iam` (owns-the-role) vs.
`iam_bindings_additive` (doesn't) split is the concrete implementation of
this exact rule.

**Won't break / scalable:** narrowing IAM roles is safe to roll out
incrementally — apply the narrower grants first, verify `terraform plan`
shows no permission-denied errors in a sandbox, then remove the broad
grants (don't remove-then-add in the same apply). The new `iam` variables
are purely additive (new optional variable, empty default) — existing
callers that don't pass `iam` are unaffected. Scales cleanly across
environments: adding a new environment/project means passing the same
`iam` map to a new `module.finops_project` call, not designing a new
permission matrix; adding a new FinOps resource type later means adding
one row to the role table, not rethinking the whole IAM model.

