## F13 — API enablement is scattered instead of owned by `modules/project`

**What we're fixing:** `test-finops-dataset/main.tf` has **8 separate
`google_project_service` resources** split across two disconnected
locations in the file — `billingbudgets`, `pubsub`, `monitoring` in one
block, and `cloudbuild`, `cloudfunctions`, `run`, `eventarc`,
`artifactregistry` in a second block added later ("Additional APIs needed
for Cloud Functions 2nd gen"). Neither block calls `modules/project` at
all — `modules/project` isn't referenced anywhere in
`test-finops-dataset`.

**Why we need this fix:** `modules/project` **already owns this exact
responsibility** — it has a working `activate_apis` variable that does
`for_each` over a list and creates `google_project_service` per API. Right
now the same job is duplicated by hand, split across two unrelated
locations, making it easy to add a new API in one place and forget the
dependency chain elsewhere (this already happened once — the second
block's own "Additional APIs needed" comment is the tell that the first
block was incomplete when written).

**Confirmed against CFF directly** (checked CFF's `modules/project/main.tf`,
and confirmed `bigquery-dataset`, `pubsub`, `cloud-function-v2`, and
`billing-account` have **zero** `google_project_service` resources between
them): CFF enables APIs in exactly one place —
`google_project_service.project_services`, a single `for_each` over a
`variable "services" { type = list(string) }` inside the `project`
module, sequenced after project creation and org-policy application, with
every other resource inside the project module that needs an API
depending on it via `depends_on`. No other CFF module — not
`bigquery-dataset`, not `pubsub`, not `cloud-function-v2` — ever enables
its own APIs; they all assume the calling root enabled what they need via
the `project` module first. **This is the rule this stage should follow:
API enablement is a project-lifecycle concern, owned exclusively by the
project module, done once, before any resource module runs — never
duplicated or scattered per-resource.** (Naming note: CFF calls the
variable `services`; this repo's `modules/project` already calls the
equivalent `activate_apis` — functionally identical, no fix needed for the
name itself.)

**How to fix:**
1. Add a call to `module "project"` in `test-finops-dataset/main.tf` (or,
   if the project is confirmed pre-existing/shared and out of this
   stage's ownership, at minimum consolidate all 8 resources into one
   `locals.apis` list + a single `for_each` block in one file) — either
   way, one list, one place.
2. Pass the full list in one shot:
   ```hcl
   activate_apis = [
     "billingbudgets.googleapis.com", "pubsub.googleapis.com",
     "monitoring.googleapis.com", "cloudbuild.googleapis.com",
     "cloudfunctions.googleapis.com", "run.googleapis.com",
     "eventarc.googleapis.com", "artifactregistry.googleapis.com",
     "secretmanager.googleapis.com", # needed for F9's fix
   ]
   ```
3. Update every `depends_on` currently pointing at
   `google_project_service.<name>` to point at `module.finops_project`
   instead.
4. Pass the new `iam` variable from **F7** in the *same* module call — one
   `module "project"` block becomes the single place that knows everything
   needed to stand up a usable, correctly-permissioned project, matching
   CFF's model exactly.
5. **Going forward, treat any new finops module that tries to enable its
   own APIs as a finding, not a pattern to repeat** — if a future
   `finops-*` module (or the new `finops-function` from F8) adds its own
   `google_project_service` resource instead of requiring its APIs be
   listed in the caller's `activate_apis` list, that module is violating
   this same rule and should be fixed the same way.

**AGENTS.md reference:**
> "Check the repo root for an existing module directory covering the same
> or adjacent resource before creating a new one... Extend an existing
> module if the resource fits its stated responsibility."
— `agents.md`, "Before creating or editing a module". `modules/project`
already covers "project + its APIs"; re-implementing that inline in
`test-finops-dataset` instead of calling it is the exact anti-pattern this
line warns against.

**Won't break / scalable:** `google_project_service` resources are
idempotent — moving existing API-enable calls into
`module.finops_project`'s `for_each` targets the same underlying GCP
resources, so either accept a one-time `terraform state mv` per API
resource, or let Terraform recreate the enablement (harmless — enabling an
already-enabled API is a no-op on GCP's side). The new `iam.tf` in
`modules/project` (F7) is purely additive. Every future finops resource
that needs an API just adds one string to the one `activate_apis` list —
no new files, no new module, no re-deriving where things belong.

